1Q.What is Linux Operating System (OS)? List three pros and cons of 
it. What is Linux Operating System (OS)? 
Linux is a free and open-source Unix-like operating system based on 
the Linux kernel, first released by Linus Torvalds in 1991. It is widely 
used in servers, desktops, embedded systems, and supercomputers. 
Linux distributions (distros) — such as Ubuntu, Fedora, and Debian — 
package the kernel with system software and applications to create a 
complete OS. Linux is known for its stability, security, and flexibility. 
Three Pros of Linux: 
1. Open Source & Free 
Linux is free to use, modify, and distribute. Its open-source 
nature allows users to inspect, customize, and improve the 
code, fostering innovation and community-driven development. 
2. High Security & Stability 
Linux is less prone to malware and viruses compared to other 
OSes. It’s known for running for long periods without crashing, 
making it ideal for servers and mission-critical systems. 
3. Lightweight & Highly Customizable 
Many Linux distros are lightweight and can run efficiently on 
older or low-resource hardware. Users can tailor the system’s 
appearance, behaviour, and components to suit their needs. 
Three Cons of Linux: 
1. Steeper Learning Curve 
New users may find Linux intimidating due to its reliance on the 
command line and different file system structure, especially if 
coming from Windows or macOS. 
2. Limited Software & Hardware Support 
Some popular commercial software (e.g., Adobe Photoshop, 
Microsoft Office) and certain hardware drivers (especially for 
gaming peripherals or printers) may not be fully supported or 
require workarounds. 
3. Fragmentation & Inconsistent User Experience 
With hundreds of distributions and desktop environments, 
Linux can feel fragmented. Support, updates, and UI/UX may 
vary widely between distros, confusing beginners 
. 
2Q.Differentiate between Linux, Mac, Android, and Windows OS 
with at least six unique features. 
Linux OS 
1. Open Source & Community-Driven 
Entirely open-source; users can view, modify, and redistribute 
the source code. Development is driven by global communities 
and organizations (e.g., Debian, Red Hat). 
2. Highly Customizable 
Users can choose from hundreds of distributions (Ubuntu, Arch, 
Fedora) and desktop environments (GNOME, KDE, XFCE) to 
tailor the OS to their needs. 
3. Strong CLI (Command Line Interface) Focus 
Advanced users heavily rely on terminal commands for system 
management, automation, and development — offering 
granular control. 
4. Excellent for Servers & DevOps 
Dominates server, cloud, and container environments (e.g., 
AWS, Docker, Kubernetes) due to stability, security, and low 
resource usage. 
5. No Licensing Costs 
Free to download, install, and use — even for commercial 
purposes — reducing TCO (Total Cost of Ownership). 
6. High Security & Low Malware Risk 
Built with strong user permission models and less targeted by 
malware due to smaller desktop market share and open-source 
scrutiny. 
macOS (Apple Mac OS) 
1. Exclusive to Apple Hardware 
Runs only on MacBooks, iMacs, and Mac Minis — tightly 
integrated with Apple’s ecosystem (iPhone, iPad, Apple Watch). 
2. Unix-Based with Polished UI/UX 
Built on Darwin (BSD Unix), offering Unix power under a sleek, 
intuitive, and consistent graphical interface. 
3. Optimized for Creative Professionals 
Industry standard for video/audio editing, design, and 
development (Final Cut Pro, Logic Pro, Xcode) due to 
performance and software optimization. 
4. Seamless Ecosystem Integration 
Features like Handoff, AirDrop, Universal Clipboard, and iCloud 
sync work flawlessly across Apple devices. 
5. Controlled Software Distribution (App Store + Gatekeeper) 
Apps are vetted via the Mac App Store or notarized by Apple, 
enhancing security while limiting sideloading flexibility. 
6. Regular, Cohesive Updates 
Apple delivers unified OS updates across all supported devices 
with strong backward compatibility and minimal fragmentation. 
Android OS 
1. Mobile-First, Linux-Based OS 
Built on a modified Linux kernel, but designed primarily for 
touchscreen mobile devices (smartphones, tablets, wearables). 
2. Highly Fragmented Ecosystem 
Multiple versions run simultaneously across devices from 
different manufacturers (Samsung, Xiaomi, etc.), leading to 
inconsistent UX and update delays. 
3. Google Services Integration 
Deeply integrated with Google apps (Gmail, Maps, Drive, 
Assistant) and dependent on Google Mobile Services (GMS) for 
full functionality. 
4. Extensive Hardware Compatibility 
Runs on thousands of device models with varying specs — from 
budget phones to flagship devices — enabling wide global 
adoption. 
5. Custom UI Skins & Launchers 
Manufacturers overlay custom interfaces (One UI, MIUI, 
ColorOS) and users can install third-party launchers to change 
the entire look and feel. 
6. Permission-Based App Security Model 
Apps must request specific permissions (location, camera, mic) 
— users can grant or deny them, enhancing privacy control. 
Windows OS (Microsoft) 
1. Dominant Desktop OS Worldwide 
Holds the largest market share for personal computers, making 
it the default choice for businesses, schools, and consumers. 
2. Broad Software & Game Compatibility 
Supports the widest range of commercial software (Adobe 
Suite, MS Office) and is the primary platform for PC gaming 
(DirectX, Steam, Xbox integration). 
3. Proprietary with Paid Licensing 
Closed-source OS requiring paid licenses for most versions 
(Home, Pro, Enterprise), though OEMs often pre-install it. 
4. GUI-Centric with Optional CLI Tools 
Designed for ease of use via graphical interface; advanced users 
can use PowerShell or WSL (Windows Subsystem for Linux) for 
command-line tasks. 
5. Active Directory & Enterprise Management 
Deep integration with Microsoft enterprise tools (Azure AD, 
Intune, Group Policy) makes it ideal for corporate IT 
environments. 
6. Frequent Feature Updates & Legacy Support 
Regular major updates (e.g., Windows 11) with long-term 
support for older software and hardware — sometimes leading 
to bloat or compatibility quirks. 
3Q. Why is Linux preferred for Mainframe Servers for legacy 
application? Give three out-of-the-box technical reasons. 
Linux is increasingly preferred for running legacy applications on 
mainframe servers—especially IBM Z systems—not just for cost or 
ideology, but for concrete technical advantages that align with the 
stringent demands of enterprise legacy environments. Here are three 
out-of-the-box technical reasons: 
1. Binary Compatibility via Linux on IBM Z (s390x Architecture) 
Legacy mainframe applications (e.g., COBOL, PL/I, CICS, IMS) were 
originally written for IBM’s proprietary operating systems like z/OS. 
However, Linux on IBM Z runs natively on the same s390x 
architecture, enabling: 
• Direct recompilation of legacy C/C++/COBOL code without 
emulation layers. 
• Use of z/Architecture instructions (e.g., decimal floating-point, 
cryptographic assists) that accelerate legacy 
f
 inancial/transactional workloads. 
• Seamless integration with z/OS via cross-system coupling (e.g., 
using IBM Z Common Data Provider). 
Result: Legacy apps run at near-native speed with minimal code 
changes.  
2. Enterprise-Grade RAS (Reliability, Availability, Serviceability) 
Mainframes are engineered for "five-nines" (99.999%) uptime, and 
Linux on Z inherits hardware-level RAS features: 
• Memory Mirroring & Hot-Spare CPUs: Faulty components are 
replaced without downtime. 
• Channel Subsystem Redundancy: I/O paths auto-failover during 
hardware faults. 
• First Failure Data Capture (FFDC): Detailed hardware diagnostics 
for rapid root-cause analysis. 
Result: Linux workloads on mainframes achieve the same resilience 
as traditional z/OS applications—critical for banking, insurance, and 
government systems.  
3. High-Density Virtualization with z/VM & KVM 
Mainframes excel at massive virtualization scale, and Linux leverages 
this uniquely: 
• A single IBM Z server can run >8,000 Linux virtual machines 
simultaneously via z/VM (IBM’s hypervisor) or KVM for IBM Z. 
• Legacy monolithic apps can be containerized (Docker/Podman) 
or VM-isolated without performance overhead. 
• Live Guest Relocation: Move running Linux VMs between 
physical mainframes with zero downtime. 
Result: Organizations modernize legacy apps incrementally—lifting 
COBOL apps into containers while maintaining SLAs.  
Why This Matters for Legacy Apps 
These aren’t theoretical benefits—they solve real legacy challenges: 
• Avoid rewrites: Run 30-year-old COBOL on Linux without 
touching business logic. 
• Meet compliance: Leverage mainframe-grade auditability (e.g., 
pervasive encryption). 
• Scale efficiently: Handle Black Friday transaction spikes with 
sub-capacity billing. 
4Q. Explain the structure of the Linux File System with proper 
diagram. 
The Linux file system follows the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS), 
which defines the directory structure and purpose of each directory 
in a consistent way across all Linux distributions. Unlike Windows 
(which uses drive letters like C:), Linux uses a single inverted tree 
rooted at / (the root directory). 
Below is a clear explanation of key directories, followed by a visual 
tree diagram generated using the tree command. 
A. Main Directories in Linux File System 
• / (Root): The top-most directory. Everything starts here. 
• /bin: Essential user binaries (basic commands like ls, cp, mv). 
• /boot: Boot loader files, kernel images required for system 
startup. 
• /etc: Configuration files for the system and applications. 
• /home: User’s personal directories (/home/user1, 
/home/user2). 
• /lib: Shared libraries required by system programs. 
• /sbin: System binaries for administrators (e.g., shutdown, 
mount). 
• /usr: Secondary hierarchy for user applications, libraries, 
documentation. 
• /var: Variable files (logs, spools, caches). 
• /tmp: Temporary files (cleared on reboot). 
• /dev: Device files (e.g., hard drives /dev/sda, terminals). 
• /proc: Virtual directory with process and kernel info. 
• /mnt & /media: Mount points for external drives (USB, 
CD/DVD). 
