5Q.If Linux OS is open-source, how do companies like Red Hat still 
making money from it? Do a market study and answer properly 
Core Paradox Explained 
Linux is open-source (freely usable, modifiable, and distributable 
under the GNU GPL license). Yet companies like Red Hat, SUSE, and 
Canonical generate billions in revenue. 
How? They don’t sell the software—they sell enterprise-grade 
services, support, and certified solutions built around open-source 
Linux. 
Red Hat’s Business Model: The "Open Source Subscription" 
1. Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) Subscriptions 
• What customers pay for: 
• Certified, stable, and tested Linux builds (not raw 
community code). 
• 10+ years of security patches & bug fixes (critical for 
banks, governments). 
• Hardware/Software Certification: Guaranteed 
compatibility with Oracle DB, SAP, NVIDIA GPUs, etc. 
• 24/7 enterprise support with SLAs (e.g., 1-hour response 
for critical issues). 
• Compliance & Audit Tools: FIPS 140-2, DISA STIG, HIPAA, 
GDPR-ready configurations. 
Key Insight: Enterprises cannot risk using untested community Linux 
in production. They pay for accountability.  
2. Hybrid Cloud & Middleware Stack 
Red Hat monetizes adjacent open-source ecosystems: 
• OpenShift: Kubernetes platform ($1.3B+ annual revenue). 
• Ansible Automation: IT automation ($500M+ revenue). 
• RHEL for SAP, Microsoft Azure, AWS: Cloud-optimized 
subscriptions. 
3. Training & Certification 
• RHCE (Red Hat Certified Engineer): $400–$800/exam. 
• On-site workshops: $10K–$50K/session for enterprises. 
