Key Advantages / Technical Differentiators That Make Customers 
Willing to Pay 
From a more technical / operational viewpoint, here are reasons 
enterprises choose to pay even though the software is open: 
1. Guaranteed Security & Updates 
With RHEL, you get certified, tested patches; compliance with 
industry/regulatory security standards; predictable release 
schedules. For mission-critical systems, that reliability matters. 
2. Stability & Certification 
Versions are certified with hardware, software stacks. The OS is 
tested under many scenarios. Enterprises don’t want to constantly 
deal with breaking changes. They use long-term support (LTS) 
releases. 
3. Support Agreements & SLAs 
If something breaks (especially in production), having a vendor 
support agreement (SLA) is vital. They want 24x7 support, 
guarantees about fixes, and escalation paths which free or 
community-versions may not offer. 
4. Integration & Ecosystem Compatibility 
Enterprises often have heterogeneous environments. Having 
compatibility with major hardware, virtualization platforms, third
party software, cloud providers, containers etc. simplifies 
operations. 
Risks / Challenges 
Red Hat (and similar companies) also face risks, which affect how 
their business model needs to evolve: 
• Free / community alternatives (e.g. CentOS in the past; forks, 
etc.) that try to reproduce compatibility without cost. 
• Cloud providers offering their own Linux distributions or 
managed services which may undercut traditional enterprise 
deals. 
• The pace of innovation required (containers, hybrid cloud, AI, 
edge computing) to stay relevant. 
